      "dom_id": "question-1133",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that  $\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-4x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-3x)$, what is the value of $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-4x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-3x) \\\\ 10-4x & =10-3x \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-4(0)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(10-3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(10) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.",
