      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-5)$ for $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-5) \\\\ 3x+1 & =x-5 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-5) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(-3)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-3-5) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-8) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-8)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions.",
